hey Greg knuckles here with string  theory calm and today we're talking  about the bench press  specifically strength out of the bottom  portion of the bench press and options  to give you a longer range of motion  specifically the question I was asked  was about flat-backed bench a lot of  geared ventures tend to bench for all  more often in the offseason to  strengthen their bench off the chest  because that's something that is  neglected and geared work so the  question was would benching with a flat  back as opposed to an arch do the same  thing for a role venture and potentially  it would but I still wouldn't recommend  it simply because there are better  options it basically comes down to a  risk to reward calculus and it's not  that benching with a flat back is  inherently risky it might be more so for  some people than others  but there are clearly a lot of people  who have benched with a flat back their  entire lives and never got shoulder  problems Jennifer Thompson is a good  example she has a relatively flat back  to pinch and is absurdly strong  I think she's benched 308 at 132 and has  never had any shoulder problems that I'm  aware of Mike McDonald who is a great  venture of yesteryear he he benched I  think 606 at 242 and 582 at 220 he was  also a relatively flat back bencher  never had any shoulder problems that I'm  aware of  so yeah you can definitely bench some  people can definitely bench with a flat  back and never have shoulder issues but  some people will so I wouldn't recommend  flat back benches just as a general  recommendation simply because for some  people they do carry risks and there are  other options that can help you  accomplish the same thing that flat back  benches would that don't carry the same  risks so that's what I'll be talking  about in this video so number one is the  close grip bench  a lot of people think of the Close Grip  primarily as a triceps exercise but when  you actually think about it when your  grip moves them a little closer  your elbow ends up being a bit lower  than the bars on your chest versus if  your grip is wider so close grip bench  een actually helps you work through that  longer range of motion as well the next  thing would be floor press that doesn't  necessarily give you a longer range of  motion but it forces you to start the  press from a dead stop without any  assistance from your legs and most  people are actually a little bit weaker  a couple inches off the chest then right  on the chest so that weakness out of the  bottom of a bench floor press can help a  lot of people with that the next one is  Paul's benching if you're not already  doing pause benches and you're a power  lifter then you probably should because  you need to do them for competition if  you're not looking to compete and you're  used to just benching touch-and-go then  pitching with the short pause can help  you develop some of that strength out of  the bottom if you're used to benching  with the pause than benching with a  slightly longer one can serve the same  purpose the next one is what's been  called lately this photo press after  Eric's photo who has the all-time world  record benchpress at super heavy weight  but people have been doing this exercise  forever basically it's a polished bench  press but instead of pausing with the  bar on your chest you let it hover about  half an inch off of your chest so you  actually have to continue controlling  the weight and you can't actually let it  rest on your chest and let your body  support the weight the next one is  benching with your feet up again you can  still do this with an arch it can be the  exact same bench stroke except you have  to actually use your pecs triceps  shoulders to produce more of the force  to lift the bar off of your chest since  you don't have the assistance of leg  drugs and the next one is pin press with  the bar starting right around your chest  either barely touching your chest or  barely hovering off of it you can still  use leg drive with this but what it does  is since you're not lowering the weight  beforehand you don't have that  Rett reflux and the elastic energy from  the eccentric part of the muscle to help  you reverse the weight so it also helps  build strength out of the hole there are  two final options that aren't actually  benchpress variations that serve this  purpose very well also the first is  dumbbell press because with a dumbbell  you don't have to worry about the bar  stopping you right here you can keep  going a little bit lower and unlike flat  back bench you're not locked into a  pronated hand position you can do them  with a neutral grip so if it does start  causing shoulder discomfort you can  instead of dumbbell benching here as you  lower it you can do a neutral grip to  take some of that stress off of your  shoulders and another one for similar  reasons would be dips again because you  can do them with a neutral grip a more  shoulder friendly option that still  allows for a longer range of motion  obviously don't take any of this as  physical therapy or rehab advice or  anything like that I'm just a dude who  lifts heavy weights giving you advice  about lifting heavy weights so all of  these options I just went through today  I see them as things with the same  potential upside as flat back bench that  carry fewer risks but obviously none of  them are completely risk-free as making  up heavy things is not completely  risk-free and some of them may cause you  more discomfort than others so I give  you a pretty long list of options so  just go with the ones that are more  comfortable for you because they all  accomplish basically the same purpose so  if one of them is more comfortable in  one of them hurts obviously go with the  one that's more comfortable alright guys  so this was a Q&amp;A video I'm going to  start doing these more often so if you  have questions ask them in the comments  below if I like them then I'll respond  to them if I don't then I probably won't  respond to them but I will appreciate  that you asked so that's it guys please  like and subscribe thanks for watching
 

